          "text": "A CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launches primarily because it eliminates the CPU-side overhead associated with the CUDA driver and runtime. When you launch kernels eagerly, the CPU must perform a significant amount of work for every single kernel: it validates parameters, allocates resources, and communicates with the driver to schedule the work. This creates a bottleneck where the GPU often sits idle waiting for the CPU to catch up, especially when running many small kernels. By capturing a sequence of operations into a graph, the driver pre-validates the entire sequence and optimizes the scheduling logic. When the graph is replayed, the CPU simply issues a single command to execute the pre-compiled structure, allowing the GPU to launch kernels back-to-back with minimal latency.\n\nFurthermore, graphs allow the CUDA runtime to optimize the execution flow in ways that eager launches do not. Because the entire dependency structure of the kernels is known upfront, the runtime can perform optimizations such as reducing synchronization overhead between kernels and minimizing the time the GPU spends in idle states between operations. This is particularly beneficial for workloads with complex dependency chains or high-frequency kernel launches, where the cumulative savings of reduced launch latency result in significant throughput improvements.\n\nHowever, this performance gain comes with specific costs. The primary cost is the memory required to store",
